<metadata>
    <!--
        The element name below is the widget id. Core globs this directory for *.xml and uses that
        id as the key of the widget in every user's saved dashboard layout, so renaming it would
        silently drop the widget off the dashboards it is already on. It stays "linkhealth".

        Every string in <translations> is passed through gettext() by the dashboard controller, so
        the English text here is the msgid the plugin's i18n/ui catalogues translate.

        NOTHING goes inside <translations>, including a comment. The controller does

            $translations = (array)($metadataAttributes->translations ?? []);
            foreach ($translations as $key => $value) { $translations[$key] = gettext($value); }

        (V: Core/Api/DashboardController.php:124,136 on OPNsense 26.7.4_1), and an XML comment in
        there arrives as a "comment" key like any other child. ONE comment is a SimpleXMLElement,
        which gettext() coerces through __toString(), so it works and nothing says otherwise - the
        comment that used to sit beside <faceplate> below was exactly that. TWO or more are an
        array, gettext() raises a TypeError, and get_dashboard answers

            {"errorMessage":"Unexpected error, check log for details"}

        That API is what the whole Lobby is built from, so every widget on every dashboard
        disappears and the page shows one red box. Measured on the reference appliance, 2026-09-22,
        while writing the metadata for os-fanctl: three comments in that element emptied the
        dashboard, and moving them out brought it back.

        So this file had one comment's worth of margin and no warning sign. The note that was in
        there is below, the element holds strings only, and the next person to add a comment can
        put it anywhere but there.

        ==== the faceplate strings ============================================================

        The strip has no room for a caption without costing the widget a whole row, so the two
        things a drawing has to admit to are said where they cost nothing: the way round it is
        drawn goes in the label a screen reader reads out, and the fact that nobody has yet stood
        in front of the machine and checked it goes on every socket the drawing places.
    -->
    <linkhealth>
        <filename>LinkHealth.js</filename>
        <link>/ui/linkhealth</link>
        <endpoints>
            <endpoint>/api/linkhealth/service/*</endpoint>
        </endpoints>
        <translations>
            <title>Link Health</title>
            <state_ok>clean</state_ok>
            <state_watch>a few frames are being corrupted</state_watch>
            <state_warn>needs attention</state_warn>
            <state_fail>failing</state_fail>
            <state_idle>not enough traffic to judge</state_idle>
            <state_down>link is down</state_down>
            <state_disabled>port is disabled</state_disabled>
            <state_absent>the drawing shows a socket this machine does not report</state_absent>
            <faceplate>The front panel, as you face the front of the appliance</faceplate>
            <unconfirmed>position not confirmed against the machine yet</unconfirmed>
            <downshift>Negotiating below the fastest speed this port advertises</downshift>
            <unit_ppm>ppm</unit_ppm>
            <unit_dbm>dBm</unit_dbm>
            <nodata>No link health data yet. The collector runs once a minute.</nodata>
            <stale>Link health was last measured at %s and is no longer being updated.</stale>
            <unreachable>Could not read the link health status.</unreachable>
        </translations>
    </linkhealth>
</metadata>
